WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.810
When you think space, you probably think

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:11.403
one space agency that travels
under the radar is ESA.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.080
Which is surprising considering

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.630
what the European Space
Agency has achieved

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:22.453
and how vital it is to
the global space industry.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.080
We've collaborated with
pretty much everybody

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.653
who is involved in space exploration.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.230
But ESA wants to be a leading force

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:35.577
in furthering humanity's
mission into space.

00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:38.420
For the first time,
we're playing a key role

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.350
in a human space flight mission

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.393
that goes beyond low Earth orbit.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.070
Commander Ground, do you copy?

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:46.240
Copy.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.340
No sampling.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.450
If we want to have successful
human missions to Mars,

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.210
we are going to have to
learn to live and work

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.863
off-world in a different way.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.610
The place where we are
going to learn to do that

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:01.443
is on the Moon.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.070
In a world where working together

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.363
seems to be getting
more and more difficult,

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.273
can ESA help humanity
take its next giant leap?

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.870
Established in 1975, ESA
touches most corners of Europe.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:28.583
Unlike other agencies,
it's a diverse collective.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.190
The European Space Agency

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:32.970
is an inter-governmental organization

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.780
with 22 member states from Europe.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.330
The core to ESA's success

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.203
is that international collaboration.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.430
Its formative years were mainly spent

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:44.500
working in conjunction with NASA

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.970
and its record in space is pretty special.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.780
From launching the world's
first high orbit telescope

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.060
and studying comets up close,

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.640
perhaps its most famous mission

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:57.040
was launched with NASA in 1990.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.550
We joined with NASA to build
the Hubble Space Telescope

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.360
and of course that's been flying now

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.290
for more than 30 years.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.550
Hubble has since discovered

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.980
more distant objects than
any terrestrial instrument.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:13.020
In 2003, ESA launched
the Mars Express orbiter,

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.470
the first fully European
mission to a planet.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.360
Since then, there's been the
farthest landing from Earth

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.790
made on Saturn's moon Titan,

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.043
and the first robotic landing on a comet.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.910
ESA has also invested in
Earth observation satellites,

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:35.970
telecommunications and even
built Europe's own GPS system.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.660
But its astronauts haven't journeyed

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:39.550
beyond the 400 kilometer orbit

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.740
of the International Space Station.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.510
We've never played a key role

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:48.510
until now in human exploration
beyond low Earth orbit.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.900
But with the Artemis
mission, which will enable us

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.500
to send humans back to the
Moon for the first time,

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.740
ESA is a central part of that.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:01.660
ESA has built the service
module of the Orion Crew Vehicle

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.500
and the NASA and ESA astronauts

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.800
that will ride on that capsule to the Moon

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.293
will be powered by European technology.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.900
In 2019, ESA ministers

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:16.080
committed to its biggest ever budget,

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.010
an approved and ambitious
portfolio of space programs.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.500
While ESA cannot compete
with the likes of NASA

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.280
when it comes to spending,

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:27.490
ESA provides valuable science
services that have made it

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:30.623
an essential partner in
NASA's Artemis program.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.140
ESA will contribute habitation
and refueling modules

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.133
along with enhanced communications.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:41.480
It'll also be responsible
for operating elements

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:42.830
on the Lunar Gateway,

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:45.433
a permanent station in
orbit around the Moon.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.820
The Lunar Gateway has
two functions really.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:54.110
It's the first place that humans will go

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.900
to live and work for extended
periods beyond Earth's orbit.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.210
So we're really exposing humans

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:01.260
to a location which is in deep space

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.970
where the environment and the conditions

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.610
are very different from what
we find on the space station.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.200
This gives us a test
bed, somewhere to learn

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.840
how to live and work in deep space

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.310
for long periods of time.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.190
It's also somewhere where we can perform

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:15.540
scientific research which will prepare us

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:18.200
for future exploration in deep space.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.370
We're also working on
a large lunar lander,

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:24.220
which would take cargo,
logistics, robotics

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.350
and other equipment to
the surface of the Moon

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:28.340
as a contribution to the human missions

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.090
that are going to follow during the 2020s.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.110
The first human missions

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.110
to return to the surface of the Moon

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.220
are planned for the latter
part of this decade.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.050
Those could be followed by the buildup

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:41.740
of infrastructure and capability.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.890
So we could be seeing a
sustained presence on the Moon

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:46.520
into the 2020s.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.760
Sustainability is going to be based

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:52.640
on having the infrastructure
and the facilities

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.630
to enable long-term access

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.180
and if you want to do that on the Moon

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.220
in a long-term sustainable way,

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:01.130
we're going to have to
learn to use the resources

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:03.033
that we find locally wherever we can.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:05.820
If you can take the regolith,

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.820
the ground up dusty rocky powder

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.580
that we find at the lunar surface,

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:10.930
if we can then bind that together,

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:12.770
we can create structures.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:14.520
Those structures can protect us

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:16.810
from the very hot and
very cold environment,

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:18.570
so create some thermal shielding.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:21.400
We can also use it to
create radiation shielding

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.380
and so it's regular 3D printing
where you take this stuff

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.750
and you 3D print it into
the structures you need.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:29.873
It's a really exciting technology
for future applications.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.860
Gravity on the Moon is much lower

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.220
than it is on Earth,

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.760
so launching a rocket
off the lunar surface

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.270
would potentially be easier

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:40.820
as it requires much less thrust.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:42.870
Less thrust means less fuel,

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:45.570
making room in the rocket
for the essential payloads

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:48.740
to get humans to Mars and beyond.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.480
The Moon's polar regions also have

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.490
large supplies of water ice,

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:55.860
which could be broken down
into oxygen and hydrogen,

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.420
useful ingredients needed
for humans to breathe

00:05:58.420 --> 00:05:59.773
and rockets to fly.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.300
This return to the Moon
will prepare us for Mars.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.930
So we're going to be performing
cutting edge science,

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.180
the like of which has
never been done before.

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:12.700
Humans last stepped onto the Moon

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:14.500
nearly 50 years ago.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:17.960
Now, multiple nations, including
China, Russia and India

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.743
all have planned missions.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:25.060
Unlike the Apollo missions,
which lasted just days,

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.120
new missions will be much longer

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.920
because we now think the Moon
can teach us so much more.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:45.200
Off the coast of West Africa,

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:48.680
Lanzarote's volcanic landscape
might be the closest thing

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:50.933
to the lunar surface here on Earth.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:57.570
It's why ESA comes here
to run its Pangea program,

00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:01.260
a specialized international
training course for astronauts.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.510
Some of the reasons that we're
gonna go back to the Moon

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:06.770
are to look at very early
planetary system formation

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:09.360
so we can understand
the Earth Moon system.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.240
Commander Ground, we wanted to...

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:14.400
As well as Europeans,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.913
ESA is training NASA
astronaut Dr. Kate Rubins.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:22.320
Rubins has already
spent 300 days in space,

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.700
but as part of NASA's Artemis program,

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:28.510
she's likely to become the
first woman to walk on the Moon.

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:30.600
It's very stark, it's very volcanic,

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.550
and that's actually similar to the Moon.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.110
There are ancient volcanoes on the Moon.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.590
Even the regolith on the
Moon is this kind of glassy

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:38.463
volcanic rock that we see here.

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:43.970
You're looking at hundreds
of millions of years of time

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:45.820
and our hope is to discover some of these

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:47.710
very, very early rocks.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:49.700
Possibly, these rocks would be thrown up

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:52.040
through the magma near a volcano.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:53.250
So today, we are taking

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:56.350
the physical samples of
the rock and the biology,

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:58.170
but we're also taking things like spectra.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.990
So we can shoot some light at the rock

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:02.980
and see what bounces back at us

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:04.940
and different minerals
are gonna bounce back

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:06.473
different spectral patterns.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.850
The Moon's a fantastic laboratory

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:13.990
for understanding the solar system.

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:16.660
Unlike on Earth, where
we have plate tectonics,

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:18.900
we have an atmosphere, we have water,

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.530
it kind of destroys the
rock record a little bit.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.440
On the Moon, we don't have any of that.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:26.140
So actually, it's very much like it was

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:27.380
in its early history

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:29.290
and in fact, people have talked about

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:33.070
finding parts of Earth on
the Moon that are much older

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:34.730
than a lot of the rocks
we can find on Earth

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:36.400
and they're still in a more pristine state

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:39.560
because the Moon hasn't had the overturn

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.010
that you get on the earth.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:42.903
Commander Ground, do you copy?

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:45.240
The Pangea training course

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:48.160
isn't just preparing these
astronauts for the Moon.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.290
It's training them to
look for signs of life

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:53.890
that might be more likely
elsewhere in the solar system.

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:56.800
Volcanic environments
look very barren on Earth.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:58.570
There's rocks with no plants often,

00:08:58.570 --> 00:08:59.870
especially if they're very fresh

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.550
volcanic environments, very new.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:04.230
But in reality actually,
there's a lot of microbial life

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.140
living in rocks and on rocks

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:09.190
and in all sorts of parts
of a volcanic environment

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:10.130
like you can see around me.

00:09:10.130 --> 00:09:12.240
So understanding the places

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:13.880
where the most interesting biology is

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:16.550
is very important for training astronauts

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:18.960
in terms of what they should
target when they go to Mars,

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.960
and also just for scientists to understand

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.103
where we should look for
searching for life on Mars.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:27.670
This was a day where they
were letting us be autonomous.

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:29.750
So we were making some of the discoveries

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:31.780
like we would do on the lunar surface.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:33.100
So some of the things that we were finding

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:35.610
were very interesting was an area

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.470
where there was some possible
maybe hydrothermal vents

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:41.330
or some water interaction coming up

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:44.640
and we could see some
alterations in the minerals.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:47.760
So that kind of thing would
be incredibly important

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:51.010
and very valuable if we saw
that on the surface of Mars.

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:53.700
This would be an example of
a place that you would stop

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:55.823
and do a lot of sampling for life.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:58.880
The other thing that's really important

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:00.450
is the context of the sample.

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.890
So we need to know this isn't just a rock

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:05.270
that we plucked off the
ground, but where is it?

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:08.440
Is it close to the hot spring?
Is it near the volcano?

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.280
Was it an early sample?
Was it a later sample?

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:13.810
All of that kind of
metadata is really critical

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:17.850
in understanding that geo
biology sample in the context

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:21.180
of everything else that was
going on in the environment.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:23.290
The Moon could serve as a proving ground

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:25.300
for the technologies of the future,

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:28.940
helping humanity learn how
to live and work in space

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.483
and launch missions to bodies

00:10:30.483 --> 00:10:34.310
further than ever before, like Mars.

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:35.610
If you look at the surface of Mars,

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:38.370
there's lots of channels that
have been carved by water.

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.090
Early on in its history,

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:41.860
it was probably a very different planet,

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:44.910
probably a lot more habitable,
much more conducive to life.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:46.240
Potentially, there's a lot we can learn

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:49.290
about what biology is in terms of

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:50.620
kind of a feature of the universe.

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:52.040
We can learn by going to Mars

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:54.080
because perhaps it was habitable early,

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:55.350
but maybe life never developed,

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:57.230
so we need to understand why that is,

00:10:57.230 --> 00:10:59.400
or if life developed how
long it lasted there,

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:00.690
maybe it was transferred to Earth,

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:02.250
maybe it was transferred
from Earth to Mars.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.160
So there's all sorts
of different questions

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.493
that could get answered by
understanding more about Mars.

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:14.120
In ESA's Planetary Robotics Lab

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:16.170
in the Netherlands, scientists maneuver

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:19.280
a half scale version of the ExoMars rover,

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:22.113
part of a mission due to launch in 2022.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:26.170
To date, only NASA and
China have landed rovers

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:27.850
on the red planet.

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:30.173
Joining them would be a coup for ESA.

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.400
Along with several other
high-tech instruments,

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:40.020
the ExoMars rover will be
the first that can drill

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:42.200
to a depth of two meters,

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:44.660
something that could deliver
groundbreaking science

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:45.713
for the agency.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.350
We believe that material
brought from below the surface

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:55.130
has a better chance to bring
some interesting discoveries

00:11:55.130 --> 00:11:58.710
because the samples would
have been basically protected

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:01.590
by the depths from the heavy radiation

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:06.040
and the turmoil cycles
that apply on the surface

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:09.260
and which may be explain
why so far the samples

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:11.740
taken over from the surface didn't reveal

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:14.320
anything very interesting.

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:17.240
The planet is not that far from the Sun

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:19.890
and therefore the temperature
experience on the planet

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:22.610
are still adequate for
the development of life.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:25.710
The planet has also a
kind of similar region

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:28.640
and development as planet Earth

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:33.420
and therefore we can hope to
find traces of biological life

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:36.213
if we properly sound
the soil of the planet.

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:40.230
The rover was a difficult realization,

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:41.320
but I believe we are there.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:44.820
We must wield the technology
to do this sort of vehicle,

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:45.810
not only the vehicle,

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.220
but also the integration
of science instruments

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:50.760
and the realization of the
laboratory and of the drill

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:52.860
which allows to pick up the samples

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:54.533
from underneath the surface.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:57.120
ESA has suffered setbacks

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.330
with its Mars missions before,
so the stakes are high.

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:04.310
For this mission, ESA is
working with Roscosmos.

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:06.190
The Russian space agency is providing

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:07.840
the landing craft and the rocket

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:10.430
to deliver the European rover.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:12.720
Russia's involvement came after NASA said

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:14.240
it could no longer fund the purchase

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:17.750
of two U.S. Atlas V rockets of ExoMars.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:19.190
It serves as an example

00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:21.230
of what the space industry can achieve

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:23.230
through bilateral exchanges,

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:26.840
most often seen on the
International Space Station.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:29.720
That's not to say partnerships
are always easy to manage.

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:32.880
The evolution of the political world today

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:36.570
makes the setting of such
cooperation a bit more at risk.

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:38.097
If I take the example of ExoMars,

00:13:38.097 --> 00:13:40.270
the corporation was started at a point

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:43.170
when the relationship
between Europe and Russia

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:45.280
were relatively satisfactory.

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:47.500
But this relation have
worked so enormously

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:51.550
since invasion by Russia
of Crimea and Ukraine

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:53.990
and now we have basically
to deal with a partner

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:58.050
which is much more attentive
to the way we deal with them,

00:13:58.050 --> 00:14:01.280
much more secretive on the
information they reveal,

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:03.960
meaning that the risk
level of their deliverable

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.620
is increasing for us and waiting a lot

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:08.720
on the chance of success of the mission.

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:11.920
So we are basically to
take all those parameters

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:15.200
into account when setting
a corporation knowing that

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:19.270
going alone about those
big and complex mission

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:21.180
is something that so far we cannot afford

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:22.430
from a budgetary point of view.

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:25.940
So we are still in any case
to adopt cooperative good

00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:28.079
to deliver big ambitious mission,

00:14:28.079 --> 00:14:30.630
like the mission landing a rover on Mars.

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:34.070
One of my favorite phrases is
that space may be a vacuum,

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:35.760
but it doesn't take place in one,

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:37.370
meaning that politics are of course

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:40.263
an incredibly important part
of everything that we do.

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:43.160
Countries like China and India

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:45.690
are starting to challenge
the U.S's position

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:49.000
as leaders in space
technology and exploration,

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:51.070
competition which appears to be at odds

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:54.020
with ESA's core collaborative mission.

00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:56.351
Our take on the old school space race

00:14:56.351 --> 00:14:57.630
is kind of behind us.

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:00.720
The way to achieve the big
goals, which phases now,

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:02.640
whether that's looking down at the Earth

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:06.867
or looking out into the
universe, is best done together.

00:15:08.495 --> 00:15:09.980
In space exploration, at the moment,

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:11.840
I think we see a coming together

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:14.250
of two things that are a little different.

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:15.810
One is international cooperation

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:17.810
and the other is competition.

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:20.230
I think different countries,
different agencies,

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:22.930
different public and private actors

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:25.890
are aiming to have their own capability

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:29.380
to access and operate
different places in space

00:15:29.380 --> 00:15:30.213
and on the Moon.

00:15:31.640 --> 00:15:33.320
As we build these different capabilities,

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:34.750
we create competition.

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:37.300
It also drives innovation,
it drives down costs

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:39.600
and it makes it space more
accessible to everybody.

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:42.473
So I think that this is
a very positive thing.

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:46.130
Bringing together the value added

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:48.980
of working as a community
in Europe has allowed us

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:52.050
to achieve much more than
any country in Europe

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:53.600
could have achieved on its own.

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:55.560
It's not just the sum of the parts,

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:57.070
it's much greater than that.

